== Monte Carlo verification
# Monte Carlo verification of the methodology

Generated by `verification/simulate.py --trials 1000 --seed 20221110`.

## A. Multiple comparisons (look-elsewhere effect)

5 *identical* configurations, n = 40 runs each, 10 pairwise comparisons, alpha = 0.05, 1000 simulated experiments. Cell = fraction of experiments that wrongly report at least one significant difference (family-wise error rate). Theory without correction: up to 1 - 0.95^10 = 0.40.

| distribution | no correction (m = 1) | Bonferroni, per-configuration fences (paper) | Bonferroni, pooled fences (default) |
|---|---|---|---|
| normal | 0.285 | 0.084 | **0.042** |
| lognormal | 0.304 | 0.084 | **0.047** |
| shifted-exp | 0.321 | 0.237 | **0.037** |

## B. Sensitivity: detection rate vs. true difference

Two configurations, normal noise with CV = 1% (sd = 1 ms on 100 ms), Bonferroni m = 10, 500 experiments per cell. Cell = fraction in which the faster configuration is detected *and* has the correct sign.

| true difference | n = 20 | n = 40 | n = 100 |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0.05% (0.05x noise sd) | 0.01 | 0.01 | 0.01 |
| 0.1% (0.1x noise sd) | 0.01 | 0.01 | 0.01 |
| 0.25% (0.25x noise sd) | 0.02 | 0.04 | 0.14 |
| 0.5% (0.5x noise sd) | 0.10 | 0.24 | 0.74 |
| 1% (1x noise sd) | 0.51 | 0.93 | 0.99 |
| 2% (2x noise sd) | 1.00 | 1.00 | 1.00 |

## C. Randomised, interleaved execution order vs. machine drift

Two *identical* configurations, n = 40 each, noise CV = 1%, plus a slow linear drift that makes the machine 3% slower from the first to the last run (e.g. heating up). alpha = 0.05, one comparison, 1000 experiments. Cell = false-positive rate.

| execution order | false-positive rate | mean reported difference |
|---|---|---|
| sequential (A...A B...B) | **1.000** | -1.46% |
| randomised rounds (paper) | **0.005** | +0.02% |

## D. Outlier removal (1.5 x IQR)

True difference 0.5%, noise CV = 1%, n = 40; each run has a 5% chance of a +30% spike (a background job, a page-cache miss, ...). Student t-test on the raw data vs. the full pipeline (IQR filter, then normality test, then t-test or rank-sum). alpha = 0.05, one comparison, 1000 experiments.

| analysis | detection rate | mean estimated difference | false positives in an A/A test |
|---|---|---|---|
| t-test on raw data | **0.07** | +0.53% (sd 1.41) | 0.039 |
| pipeline, per-configuration fences (paper) | **0.59** | +0.50% (sd 0.25) | 0.068 |
| pipeline, pooled fences (default) | **0.57** | +0.48% (sd 0.24) | 0.045 |

## E. Is the outlier step itself valid? (type I error of one comparison)

Two *identical* configurations, n = 40 each, noise CV = 1%, alpha = 0.05, 2000 experiments. A valid procedure gives ~0.05. Spikes: 5% of runs +30%.

| distribution | per-configuration fences (paper) | pooled fences (default) | no outlier removal |
|---|---|---|---|
| normal | **0.074** | **0.053** | 0.051 |
| lognormal | **0.086** | **0.059** | 0.057 |
| shifted-exp | **0.166** | **0.050** | 0.048 |
| normal + spikes | **0.073** | **0.054** | 0.044 |

